Day16
Problem statement: Setup Nginx as a load balancer and service response from other three application servers.

1. Login into server where we need to install / configure nginx as a load balancer.

2. Install nginx using below command
        sudo yum install nginx -y   //Installation
        sudo systemctl start nginx  // starting nginx
        sudo systemctl enable nginx // Enable nginx so post restart of system nginx service is started.

3. Configure nginx.conf. Open the configuration file using a text editor (e.g., sudo vi /etc/nginx/nginx.conf). You need to add an upstream block within the http context to define your application servers and then proxy traffic to them in a server block.

4. Modify the file to look like this (replace app_server1, app_server2, app_server3 with their actual hostnames or IPs if needed, and ensure you include the correct port):
        http {
    # Define the upstream group
    upstream myapp {
        server app_server1.example.com:PORT_NUMBER;
        server app_server2.example.com:PORT_NUMBER;
        server app_server3.example.com:PORT_NUMBER;
    }

    server {
        listen 80;

        location / {
            proxy_pass http://myapp;
        }
    }
    # ... rest of the existing configuration ...
    }

5. Note: Verify the exact port your application servers are using by checking their internal configuration or running below command
        netstat -tulpn | grep http on one of the app servers.

6. Validate and Restart. After saving your changes, verify the syntax and reload Nginx to apply the configuration:
        sudo nginx -t
        sudo systemctl reload nginx

7. Verification: Once configured, you can test the load balancing by running the following command in your terminal:
        curl http://load_balancer_host:80